window.FD_DOCS=[{"t":"Docs home","u":"/docs/","g":"Start here","s":"In development. How to use Flight Day: the alarm clock first, then everything you can add, what you can change, what each screen shows, and honest fixes.","h":[],"x":"Flight Day docs In development · Early access soon How to use Flight Day day to day. The alarm clock comes first, then everything you can add as your home grows. Search the docs Getting started What it is, the screens it runs on, the five areas and how to move around on each device. The alarm clock The launch alarm, Snooze, Back to bed, the morning briefing, Goodnight and the schedule. Your whole day and home Calendar, weather, comfort, lights, the vacuum, the Systems check and the Space feed. What’s configurable Every setting by area, with its default and where to find it on each device. What am I looking at? Each area’s headline readouts, and what “as of” and the other status lines mean. Things to try Ten quick wins in today’s build, from an alarm preview to tracking a launch. Troubleshooting and FAQ Common questions and their honest fixes, without resets or guesswork. Requirements and setup What runs Flight Day today, what’s optional and what isn’t built yet. About these docs. They describe the current build: Web/Android 1.10.3 on a wall panel and Google TVs, and the iPhone, iPad and Apple Watch apps in private TestFlight testing. Mac and Apple TV apps are in development. Flight Day runs in one household today. Anything not built yet carries a label: In development or Concept . The install guide is being prepared. Until then, try the demo house : the real web app in your browser, with sample data and Sam and Riley as the crew. It never connects to a house."},{"t":"Getting started","u":"/docs/getting-started/","g":"Start here","s":"In development. What Flight Day is, the screens it runs on, the five areas, and how to move around on a touch panel, a TV remote, the Siri Remote, iPhone, iPad, Mac and Apple Watch.","h":[["screens","The screens it runs on"],["areas","The five areas"],["moving-around","Moving around"],["normal-day","A normal day"]],"x":"Getting started Flight Day is a better alarm clock, on every screen you own. A launch countdown wakes you while the screen brightens, the morning briefing follows at liftoff, and Goodnight dims the screens at night. Then it grows into your whole home dashboard and day planner as you add things: your calendar, the thermostat, lights, a robot vacuum, a Systems check for what needs attention at home, the Space feed. Each of those is optional and appears when you have it. See What you can add . The screens it runs on Screen How you use it Status Wall panel Touch. An Android panel where Flight Day is the home screen and controls the backlight at night. In daily use Google TV The remote’s D-pad, OK and Back. A bedroom TV can be the night screen and play the alarm. In daily use iPhone, iPad Native apps with touch, keyboard and widgets. Private TestFlight testing Mac Native app with keyboard and widgets. In development Apple TV The Siri Remote, with Top Shelf. In development Apple Watch The Digital Crown and complications. Private TestFlight testing Every screen has the same features, each in its own way. The wall panel and TVs share one web app; the Apple apps are native. The five areas Area Use it for Home The time, the next event or wake time, weather, Today or Tomorrow, what’s due, Sleep & Wake, what’s playing, alerts and plan limits. Space Launches, the globe, ISS passes, crew, events, news and Flight Director. House Lights and scenes, the thermostat and Smart Comfort, room readings, the vacuum, speakers, TVs and cameras. Night Tomorrow’s wake, the weekly schedule, bedtime targets, the sleep sound and recent mornings. Status What’s connected and what isn’t, plan limits and machines. Two more views open over the areas: Ambient , the idle picture, and Settings . The briefing and Flight Director open full screen. Moving around Wall panel Tap an area in the top bar. Number keys 1 – 5 also switch areas. Press and hold a Home tile to arrange Home. The gear opens Settings. Google TV remote The D-pad moves focus and OK chooses. A focused reading card scrolls. Back closes what’s open, one step at a time, then returns to Home. During an alarm, Back means Snooze. Menu arranges Home when you’re on Home, otherwise it opens Settings. Play/Pause turns Ambient on or off, except during the alarm or at night. The Home card has Back to Apple TV to switch the TV’s input back. In Settings, Right or OK enters a group. Left from the first control, or Back , returns to the list. Apple TV (Siri Remote) Swipe or press the ring to move, and press the centre to choose. Focus on the top bar switches areas. Play/Pause turns Ambient on or off, and Back closes a screen. Ambient starts after 5 idle minutes. iPhone, iPad and Mac iPhone: five tabs at the bottom. Settings is the gear on Home. iPad: tabs across the top. An iPad on the wall goes to Ambient after 10 idle minutes. Mac: a sidebar, ⌘1 – ⌘5 for the areas and ⌘, for Settings. The menu bar shows a countdown to the next liftoff. During the alarm, Esc snoozes. Apple Watch Five pages; turn the Digital Crown to move between them. On Night, the Crown nudges the wake time in 15-minute steps. Double tap to snooze is In development . A normal day Look at Home for the time and the next event or wake time. Open Night before changing the morning. Use Tomorrow only for a one-off change. Choose Goodnight when you go to bed. Only you start it; a bedtime target never does. When the alarm rings, choose Snooze or I’m up . Choose Done when you’ve read the briefing. Launch alarm Wall panel · the countdown while the screen brightens, liftoff on time, then the morning briefing Recorded from the real app (sample data)"},{"t":"The launch alarm","u":"/docs/launch-alarm/","g":"The alarm clock","s":"In development. How the launch alarm works: the ten-minute countdown, liftoff, Snooze, I’m up, Scrub, −1 min, Launch now, the must-be-up-by limit and Back to bed, on every screen.","h":[["how-it-runs","How it runs"],["controls","The controls"],["up-by","Snooze and “must be up by”"],["back-to-bed","Back to bed"],["devices","On each device"]],"x":"The launch alarm A countdown while the screen brightens, liftoff at your wake time, then the morning briefing. It’s the front door to Flight Day. Launch alarm Wall panel · the countdown while the screen brightens, liftoff on time, then the morning briefing Recorded from the real app (sample data) How it runs Ten minutes before your wake time, the count starts on the screens set to wake you. The screen brightens like a dawn; if you have smart lights, they rise too, always in their warmest colour. At T-0 the rocket lifts off and the alarm rings. The morning briefing takes over: the weather, your day and the news. The alarm sound plays on a screen: on your chosen alarm screen, starting with a quiet morning sound and a low rumble at liftoff. If a speaker is set up, the alarm raises its volume up to the alarm maximum. Other screens can provide a silent visual dawn. The controls Control What it does Snooze 10 min Moves liftoff to 10 minutes from now, then runs a one-minute count. The screen goes quiet and shows “Snoozed · liftoff” with the new time. I’m up Ends the alarm and the night. The briefing stays. A tap on touch screens; on a TV remote, hold OK while it rings. Scrub Calls off the morning. Hold it for 1.2 seconds; a short touch does nothing. The screen says “Scrubbed · alarm off today” and goes back to night. −1 min During the count only: brings liftoff one minute closer, down to the last minute. Launch now Starts a ten-second final count instead of skipping the count. On a TV remote The first key only shows the controls. It never cancels the morning by itself. Back and Play/Pause snooze. Hold OK on I’m up while it rings. Right is −1 min during the count. The TV’s Home key never ends the alarm. Snooze and “must be up by” Each morning has an up-by time: the earliest of the day’s Up by , your first timed calendar event minus a ready lead (45 minutes to start with), and your wake time plus a snooze window (30 minutes to start with). The header shows it: “liftoff 6:50 AM · up by 7:20 AM · 1 snooze”. When the next liftoff would be less than 3 minutes before up-by, Snooze disappears and the screen says “No more snoozes — up by …”. At the deadline the header reads “Final launch”. I’m up then always needs a hold. If nobody answers, the alarm stops after 15 minutes and the header reads “Up … · no answer”. Snooze length, the snooze window and the ready lead can be changed in the Apple apps (Night › Crew & snooze defaults). See What’s configurable . Back to bed After I’m up, until noon, the briefing offers Back to bed . It returns only the bedroom to night, with its sleep sound. It doesn’t set a new alarm or put the rest of the house to sleep. Snoozed night clock TV · the dim night clock shows the next liftoff Recorded from the real app (sample data) On each device Device During the alarm Bedroom TV The count, the sound and the controls. In today’s setup it’s the screen that plays the alarm. Wall panel The count and the controls; the backlight rises. No sound. Other TVs Stay out of it. iPhone A Live Activity and a notification with Snooze, I’m up and Scrub. Apple Watch A complication, with −1 min and Launch now. Apple TV and iPad Join only when set to Wake room · Bedroom screen . When only one crew member is launching, it’s a quiet launch on their phone, if it’s set up for it; otherwise the full alarm runs. Try it safely. Night › Tonight › Try the alarm runs a 45-second local preview on that screen. A preview doesn’t prove the bedroom speaker or overnight delivery work."},{"t":"The morning briefing","u":"/docs/briefing/","g":"The alarm clock","s":"In development. What the morning briefing shows at liftoff, how long it stays, and how to open it on demand.","h":[["contents","What’s in it"],["how-long","How long it stays"],["on-demand","Open it any time"],["coming","Coming next"]],"x":"The morning briefing At liftoff the countdown hands over to the day: weather, your calendar, what’s due and the news, in numbers rather than adjectives. What’s in it The header: a greeting, the time and date, and the rain hint for the next hour. Weather: the temperature and summary, “Feels like” (or RealFeel with AccuWeather), sunrise and sunset, any weather alert and a 12-hour strip. Each part names its source. Your day: Today or Tomorrow with countdowns, and what’s due today and tomorrow. Last night: only when someone shares sleep data. Space: the next launch with its T-minus, whether it’s visible from home, and the ISS when it passes high. News: a couple of headlines, space news and one AI-made line, labelled as AI. A picture of the day and, at the foot, plan limits. The briefing also shows the alarm controls while the alarm is still ringing: Snooze, I’m up and Scrub. How long it stays An alarm briefing stays until you choose Done , or until the Briefing stays up time runs out: 1 h, 2 h (the default), 3 h or until noon, counted from the original wake. I’m up and reconnecting don’t extend it. Change it in Settings › Night & alarm on a wall or TV, or Night › Crew & snooze defaults on Apple. Open it any time Tap the Weather tile on the panel, the Today tile on a TV, or a weather alert. An on-demand briefing closes after 45 minutes on the panel and 30 minutes on a TV. Coming next Go / No-Go In development (in the demo house; arrives with the next app release): one line at the top of the briefing. When an input is stale, it gives no verdict. Examples from the design: Checks incomplete · weather as of 6:10 AM Go. Dry until 6:00 PM · first event 9:30 AM No-Go: severe thunderstorm warning until 9:00 AM"},{"t":"Goodnight and the night screen","u":"/docs/goodnight/","g":"The alarm clock","s":"In development. How Goodnight puts the house and screens to bed, and how to use the dim night controls.","h":[["goodnight","Choose Goodnight"],["night-controls","The night controls"],["night-look","How each screen looks at night"]],"x":"Goodnight and the night screen One tap when you’re ready for bed: the screens dim, the sleep sound starts, and the bedroom stays dark until morning. Goodnight Wall panel · one tap powers the house down, then a touch shows the dim red night controls Recorded from the real app (sample data) Choose Goodnight Choose Goodnight on Home (Sleep & Wake) or in Night. Only you start it; a bedtime target never does. Read the summary as it runs: the lights going off, the comfort line, the sleep sound and its volume, tomorrow’s first event, the alarm and the up-by time. If Comfort asks a question, answer it yourself. With fresh air on, choose Close up or Leave it ; with the thermostat off, Resume Auto or Keep off . Nothing is chosen for you. The screens go dark. The panel’s backlight turns off; a TV shows black. Goodnight always darkens the screens. The rest depends on what you have: a speaker plays the sleep sound, smart lights go off, and a thermostat moves to its Night temperature when the comfort schedule is on. The night controls The first touch or key at night shows dim red controls for ten seconds. They show labels and values only: Tomorrow wake (this changes tomorrow only), who’s launching and up by Sound and volume, and “Muted · Select to unmute” when the speaker is muted Alarm on or off, and This screen : Pure black or Night clock Wake up now , which ends the night for the house On a remote, ▲ ▼ pick a row, ◀ ▶ adjust, OK edits or confirms and Back hides the controls. Night controls · dim red on black Sample data How each screen looks at night At night: Off (the screen turns off), Dim (a night clock stays up) or Stay on. Set it per screen. When woken: Pure black (controls only) or Night clock. Automatic picks pure black on TVs and the night clock on the panel. Without Home Assistant connected, Goodnight says so: “only this screen goes dark”. An evening look back, the Debrief, is In development ."},{"t":"The sleep schedule","u":"/docs/sleep-schedule/","g":"The alarm clock","s":"In development. Set your wake time by day, make a one-off change for tomorrow, and use bedtime targets, crew and up-by.","h":[["weekly","The weekly plan"],["tomorrow","Tomorrow only"],["bedtime","Bedtime targets"],["crew","Who’s launching and up by"],["mornings","Last night and recent mornings"]],"x":"The sleep schedule A weekly plan for waking up, a separate change for tomorrow only, and a bedtime target that never starts anything by itself. The weekly plan Open Night › Schedule , or Settings › Night & alarm › Schedule. Each group, Mon–Fri, Saturday and Sunday, has a wake time, alarm on or off and a bedtime target, shown as “bed … the night before · wake …”. Turn Customize days on to set weekdays one by one. Days that differ split out by themselves. Use −15 / +15 or the hour and minute editor, then Apply or Cancel . Tomorrow only For a one-off morning, use Tomorrow only in Night. It shows “Tomorrow: … (just once)” and leaves the weekly plan alone. Use weekly plan removes it. Changing the wake time from the night controls is also tomorrow-only. Bedtime targets Monday’s bedtime is Sunday night’s target before Monday’s wake. A target helps you see how much sleep is left. It never starts Goodnight, changes a light, starts a sound, touches the thermostat or turns a screen off. Who’s launching and up by The crew is everyone who lives in the home; each of them is a crew member. Who’s launching per day: the whole crew gets the full bedroom alarm; one crew member gets a quiet alarm on their own phone. Up by per day sets the latest you can snooze to. Leave it on Automatic to use your calendar and the snooze window. See Snooze and “must be up by” . Last night and recent mornings When a real source exists, Night shows last night (“Goodnight … · target …”) and the last seven mornings. A night recorded before targets were saved says “target not recorded” instead of borrowing today’s. Wall panel · Night Sample data · demo house Times follow the shared 12-hour or 24-hour setting. Changing the format never moves the scheduled moment."},{"t":"What you can add","u":"/docs/whole-day/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. The alarm is the front door. What Flight Day grows into as you add things: your day, comfort, lights, the vacuum, the Systems check, the Space feed, and the status of each.","h":[["core","The core"],["add","Lights up when you have it"],["today","How it’s set up today"]],"x":"What you can add The alarm clock is the front door. Add what you have, and Flight Day grows into your whole home dashboard and day planner. Each addition is optional because you add it, not because it’s minor. It appears when the device or service is there, and nothing complains when it isn’t. The core Part What it does Status Launch alarm and briefing The countdown, liftoff, Snooze, I’m up, Scrub and the morning briefing. More Core In development Goodnight and the night screen Darkens the screens and keeps the bedroom quiet. More Core In development Sleep schedule Wake times by day, tomorrow-only changes and bedtime targets. More Core In development Clock, dashboard and calendar Home: the time, the next event, Today or Tomorrow. More Core In development Weather Open-Meteo is built in, free and needs no key. More Core In development Space Launches, the globe and ISS passes from public feeds. On by default, no setup. More Optional In development Lights up when you have it Integration What it adds Status A bedroom speaker The sleep sound from Goodnight, and an alarm that raises the speaker’s volume up to a limit you set. Optional In development Lights and scenes Lights rise with the alarm and go off at Goodnight, always in their warmest colour. More Optional In development Thermostat and comfort schedule Night, Wake and Day temperatures, with Off always respected. More Optional In development Smart Comfort automatic mode Chooses heat or cool for you. It watches before it acts. Optional In development Room readings Temperature and humidity per room, with their age. Optional In development Robot vacuum Status, battery, map, Start, Pause and Dock. Cleaning one room is still being tested. More Optional In development Cameras The still from each camera’s last event; live view on Apple devices. More Optional In development TVs A Google TV runs Flight Day as its home screen, with Back to Apple TV and, on Sony, Picture off at night. Optional In development What’s due Coursework deadlines on Home and in the briefing. Optional In development AI line in the briefing One AI-made news line, labelled as AI. More Optional In development Plan limits How much of your AI plans is left this week and when each resets. Optional In development Systems check What needs attention at home: batteries, offline devices, automation errors. More Optional In development AI comfort plan A plan for the day’s temperatures, checked by the house rules before anything acts. Optional In development Flight Plan An AI-made plan for your day from your calendar and routines. Optional Concept Continue training Continue training (optional): opens the Morrowdock app's Today screen. Needs the Morrowdock iPad app (private beta). It’s a deep link only; no data from that app is shown in Flight Day. Optional In development How it’s set up today Today Flight Day runs with Home Assistant, which keeps the schedule and the house in one place and every screen in step. The integrations above come through it. See Requirements and setup . Solo flight : works without Home Assistant on one device, with the alarm, your calendar, weather and Space. In development"},{"t":"The dashboard and your day","u":"/docs/dashboard/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. Home at a glance: the clock and next event, weather, Today or Tomorrow, what’s due, Sleep and Wake, now playing, alerts and plan limits, and how to arrange the tiles.","h":[["tiles","The Home tiles"],["arrange","Arrange Home"],["calendar","Your calendar"],["planning","Planning your day"]],"x":"The dashboard and your day Home shows the day at a glance, in numbers rather than adjectives. Every tile shows real data, or says plainly what it’s waiting for. Wall panel · Home, alarm clock only Sample data · demo house The Home tiles Tile What it shows Clock and next up The time, the date and the next thing: an event, the wake time or a launch with its T-minus. Always first. Weather Now, a one-line summary and rain soon. Tap it on the panel for the briefing. Today Your calendar. After 8 PM, or once the last event has ended, it leads with Tomorrow. Due next Coursework deadlines, when connected. Sleep & Wake Tomorrow’s wake time, the alarm and Goodnight. Scenes & lights Scenes and how many lights are on. Now playing Only while something plays. Alerts Only when there are any: weather alerts and house notices. Plan limits How much of your AI plans is left, when connected. A LIVE banner appears across the top while a launch is in flight, with a Watch button. Arrange Home Press and hold a Home tile, or press Menu on a TV remote. Show or hide each tile and move it up or down. The clock always stays first. Choose Done . Reset puts the default order back. Each screen keeps its own arrangement. On iPhone, iPad and Mac it’s in Settings › Screens › Home tiles; Apple TV keeps a fixed layout. Your calendar On the wall panel and TVs, Today comes from the calendars in your Home Assistant. The Apple apps read the calendar on the device (read-only, the next 7 days). Planning your day Flight Plan, an AI-made plan for the day from your calendar and routines, is a Concept ."},{"t":"Weather and rain soon","u":"/docs/weather/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. Where Flight Day’s forecast comes from, the next-hour rain line, why it’s quiet at night, and Open in AccuWeather.","h":[["sources","Where the forecast comes from"],["rain-soon","Rain soon"],["night","Quiet at night"],["accuweather","Open in AccuWeather"],["units","Units"]],"x":"Weather and rain soon The forecast, the next hour of rain and any weather alert, each with its source named. Where the forecast comes from Open-Meteo is built in: free, with no key. If your Home Assistant has AccuWeather or Apple Weather, Flight Day prefers fresh data from those, section by section, and falls back to Open-Meteo for anything missing. The credit line names the mix, for example “AccuWeather · hourly: Open-Meteo”. RealFeel appears only with a real AccuWeather value; otherwise it says “Feels like”. Weather needs a home location. It comes from Home Assistant, or you can type it in Settings › Home location. Without one, no weather is shown. Rain soon A one-line rain forecast for the next hour, for example “Rain starting ~8:10 AM · light” or “Rain for the next 30 min, ending ~9:40 AM”. A dry forecast shows no line, on purpose. A hint that stops updating disappears after 30 minutes. It uses Apple’s next-hour forecast when the hub has one, and Open-Meteo otherwise. When Apple data is shown, its attribution and legal link are shown too. Quiet at night Bedroom screens hide rain hints at night. Living-room screens keep them. Open in AccuWeather Opens the AccuWeather app if it’s installed, or a web page with Close and Back. It doesn’t change where the dashboard gets its data. Not on Apple TV. Units °F or °C, in Settings › Home location on the wall panel and TVs."},{"t":"Smart Comfort","u":"/docs/smart-comfort/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. Heat, Cool, Off and Auto, manual holds and Resume, fresh air, the comfort schedule, room readings and the AI plan. Optional: it lights up when your thermostat is in Home Assistant.","h":[["modes","Heat, Cool, Off and Auto"],["hold","Manual hold and Resume"],["schedule","The comfort schedule"],["fresh-air","Fresh air"],["automatic","Automatic mode"],["rooms","Room readings"],["ai-plan","The AI plan"]],"x":"Smart Comfort and the thermostat Your thermostat, with a schedule that follows your night and morning. It lights up when your thermostat is in Home Assistant. Off always stays off until you say otherwise. Optional In development Smart Comfort Wall panel · thermostat controls with sample data Recorded from the real app (sample data) Heat, Cool, Off and Auto House › Thermostat has Auto , Heat , Cool and Off , plus − and +. Auto hands the thermostat back to Smart Comfort. It isn’t a thermostat mode. With the thermostat Off, the setpoint shows “—” and − and + are locked: “Choose Heat or Cool to see its setpoint”. Manual hold and Resume Pressing − or + starts a manual hold (“Setpoint · manual hold”). It lasts until the next period, or 1, 2 or 4 hours, as set in Manual changes last . Resume ends a hold early. A manual Off stays off until you choose Resume Auto . The comfort schedule Three temperatures: Night when you choose Goodnight, Wake ten minutes before your wake time, and Day from the time Day starts. Edit them in House › Thermostat, Night › Tonight or Settings. The schedule only changes the setpoint; it never turns the system on or off. Fresh air When the weather outside is right, Flight Day can suggest opening up. Fresh air mode starts it, Close up · Auto ends it and hands back to Auto, and Keep open 1 h extends it. You can also dismiss a suggestion: Not today, Just off, Keep off or Leave it. Automatic mode Smart Comfort’s automatic mode is In development . While it learns, it watches only: “Auto is watching only. Manual Heat, Cool and Off still work.” Ending the watch never clears a manual Off. Room readings In development Each room shows its temperature and humidity with its age (“3 min ago”) or “Waiting for a reading”. Old readings are dimmed and marked “not counted”. Until readings arrive, the card says “Room sensors · waiting for Apple Home”. The AI plan In development An AI-made plan for the day’s temperatures, checked by the house rules before anything acts. It shows “Plan · …” with Why and Skip today . A plan shown while the system is manually Off hasn’t been applied. At night, bedroom screens say “Comfort is quiet for the night”."},{"t":"Lights, speakers and cameras","u":"/docs/house/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. Lights and scenes in their warmest colour, speakers and TVs, and camera stills. Optional: each appears when Home Assistant has it.","h":[["lights","Lights and scenes"],["speakers","Speakers and what’s playing"],["tvs","TVs"],["cameras","Cameras"]],"x":"Lights, speakers and cameras House shows the lights, scenes, speakers, TVs and cameras your Home Assistant has, and what each one reports. Each appears only when it’s there. Optional In development Wall panel · House Sample data Lights and scenes Each light has a toggle and a level. All on and All off cover the lot. Scenes run your Home Assistant scenes and scripts. Turning a light on asks for its warmest colour. Nothing in Flight Day sets white light, and the alarm raises brightness, never colour temperature. Speakers and what’s playing Now playing shows the title, transport and volume for the selected player. Speakers show their state and can be grouped. A bedroom speaker can play the sleep sound from Goodnight, and the alarm raises it to the volume limit you set (Settings › Night & alarm). TVs TVs show their state, input and volume. On a Google TV that runs Flight Day, the Home card has Back to Apple TV , and a Sony with Picture off set up goes dark at night while it keeps playing the sleep sound. Cameras Each camera shows the still from its last event. Tap for live view on Apple devices. The wall panel and TVs don’t promise live video from a source that hasn’t been checked."},{"t":"Robot vacuum","u":"/docs/vacuum/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. The robot vacuum card: status, battery, map, Start, Pause and Return to dock, rooms and maintenance. Optional: it appears when Home Assistant has a vacuum.","h":[["card","The card"],["rooms","Cleaning one room"],["maintenance","Maintenance"]],"x":"Robot vacuum Status, battery and the map, with Start, Pause and Return to dock. It appears when your Home Assistant has a vacuum. Roborock gets room cleaning and maintenance through its own adapter. Optional In development The card The status and battery (“82% battery”), and the latest map. Start , Pause and Return to dock act on the real vacuum. Home shows only “Cleaning…” or “Stuck · …”, and a “Needs a hand” chip when it needs you. Cleaning one room In development Clean a room lists the rooms the vacuum reports. Rooms with the same name show their number. It has been tested against a simulated vacuum so far. Maintenance The main brush, side brush, filter, sensors and strainer, each with the time left or “Overdue”. An item at or below zero is overdue. Nothing resets by itself."},{"t":"Systems check and the AI planner","u":"/docs/house-check/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. What’s built and what’s coming in Flight Day’s AI features: the AI line in the briefing, plan limits, the Systems check, the AI comfort plan and Flight Plan.","h":[["today","Built today"],["house-check","Systems check"],["comfort-plan","AI comfort plan"],["flight-plan","Flight Plan"],["privacy","Where AI requests go"]],"x":"Systems check and the AI planner AI helps around the edges: a line in the briefing today, and more as it’s ready. Every AI-made line says so. Built today AI line in the briefing In development : one AI-made news line, labelled as AI, with “more ›” for the rest. Plan limits In development : how much of your AI plans is left this week and when each resets, on Home and in Status. A limit the service doesn’t report is never made up. Systems check In development What needs attention at home: batteries, offline devices, automation errors. Each finding is a Caution or a Warning . When there’s nothing, it says “Nothing needs you”, and “not run yet today” before its first run. Integrations report findings too (see Build your own ). AI comfort plan In development A plan for the day’s temperatures made from the forecast and the rooms, checked by the house rules before anything acts. See Smart Comfort . Flight Plan Concept An AI-made plan for your day from your calendar and routines. Where AI requests go Flight Day has no AI service of its own. When released, AI features go through the AI provider you choose in your Home Assistant. See Privacy ."},{"t":"The Space feed","u":"/docs/space/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. Launches with live T-minus, the globe with the ISS, passes and LOOK UP, visible-from-home launches, Flight Director, Watch and Track. Optional, on by default, no setup.","h":[["launches","Launches and the globe"],["iss","The ISS and LOOK UP"],["flight-director","Flight Director"]],"x":"The Space feed Every launch on a live globe, from public launch data. It’s on by default and needs no setup; turn the area off per screen if you don’t want it. Optional In development Space Living-room Google TV · the globe flies to each launch picked from the manifest Recorded from the real app (sample data) Launches and the globe Open Space and pick a launch from the manifest. The globe flies to its pad. The brief below shows the vehicle, pad and T-minus, and “Visible from home: look …” when that can be worked out from your location. Choose Watch . Before the stream is live, Flight Director shows the count, the pad and the published timeline. Choose Track to keep a mission selected on every screen. Settings › Stop tracking lets it go. Status comes from the launch itself. A late launch time isn’t proof that a rocket is flying. The ISS and LOOK UP The globe shows the station’s past and predicted track, launch pads and home, with a flat map if the globe can’t run. The pass box says “Next pass … · max 54° · 11 min”. During a visible pass, a LOOK UP card points to the station on Ambient and the night controls. Flight Director For the first nine minutes after a confirmed liftoff it shows altitude, speed and pitch labelled “Estimated · typical ascent, not telemetry”. Pad weather appears only when a real forecast covers the launch time. Wall panel · Space (feed: Real · as of 2026-11-14) Sample data · demo house"},{"t":"Ambient","u":"/docs/ambient/","g":"Your whole day and home","s":"In development. The idle view: the clock and the day over a NASA picture, Still, Animated or Live, when it starts and how to leave it.","h":[["modes","Still, Animated or Live"],["when","When it starts"],["leave","Leave Ambient"]],"x":"Ambient When nobody’s looking, a screen shows the time, the date, the temperature, the next event and the wake time over a picture, with its credit. Ambient Wall panel · the clock and the day over a generated sky (Concept) Recorded from the real app (sample data) Still, Animated or Live Still (the default): a picture from NASA’s libraries or the day’s launch. Animated : the same picture drifting slowly. Reduce Motion stops the drift. Live : a muted live stream when there is one, otherwise “no live stream right now · animated meanwhile”. Apple TV offers a LIVE · Watch button instead. Set it per screen in Settings › Ambient, or Settings › Screens. When it starts After 5 idle minutes on the wall panel and TVs (Settings › Ambient: never, or 1 to 30 minutes), 5 minutes on Apple TV and 10 minutes on an iPad on the wall. Near a featured launch, Flight Director takes over. At night and during the alarm, Ambient stays out of the way. Leave Ambient Touch the screen or press any key. On a TV, Play/Pause turns Ambient on and off. When something plays in another room, Ambient names only the source and the room, never the title."},{"t":"What’s configurable","u":"/docs/settings/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. Every Flight Day setting by area, with its choices, default and where to find it on a wall panel, TV, iPhone, iPad, Mac, Apple TV and Watch.","h":[["good-to-know","Good to know"],["display","Display and time"],["home","Home"],["alarm","Alarm and wake"],["night","Night and Goodnight"],["briefing","Briefing"],["comfort","Comfort and the thermostat"],["weather","Weather"],["space","Space and launches"],["ambient","Ambient and pictures"],["screens","Screens"],["apple","Apple only"],["demo","Demo house"]],"x":"What’s configurable Every setting, grouped by area, with its choices and where to find it on a wall panel or TV and on Apple devices. The default is in bold. Who a setting applies to. House settings live in Home Assistant and are the same on every screen. This screen settings belong to one wall panel, TV or Apple device; with the hub they follow that screen and can be edited from any screen in Settings › Screens. House defaults are the starting values for a new setup. A house that’s already running keeps its own values. Good to know Times are 12-hour , and every screen follows the house unless you change it. The briefing stays up 2 hours after the wake time, or until you choose Done. Snooze is 10 minutes . With Up by on Automatic, you’re up within 30 minutes of the wake time, or 45 minutes before your first event, whichever comes first. The alarm starts at 7:00 AM Mon–Fri , with weekends at 8:30 AM and their alarm off. Bedtime targets start at the wake time minus 8 hours. Lights always use their warmest colour. That isn’t a setting; it’s a rule. The comfort schedule starts off . Display and time Setting What it does Choices Where Time format House Clock style for every screen that follows the house. 12-hour · 24-hour · Both (“07:45 (7:45 AM)”) Wall/TV: Settings › Display Apple: Settings › Display › House time format This screen’s time format This screen Overrides the house format on one screen. Follow shared · 12-hour · 24-hour · Both. Apple adds System Wall/TV: Settings › Display Apple: Settings › Display Brightness This screen How bright the screen is. Auto (follows the room’s light) · 5–100% Wall/TV: Settings › Display or Screens Apple: Settings › Screens Theme This screen How the dashboard looks. Tokyo Night · Starship · Liquid Glass · Console Wall/TV: Settings › Appearance or Screens Apple: Settings › Screens › Theme Units This screen Temperature units. °F · °C Wall/TV: Settings › Home location Apple: Not on Apple yet Home location This screen The place used for weather, sunrise and sunset, and ISS passes. From Home Assistant , or typed in Wall/TV: Settings › Home location Apple: Settings › Home location Home Setting What it does Choices Where Home tiles This screen Show, hide and reorder the Home tiles. The clock always stays first. All shown , in the standard order Wall/TV: Hold a tile, or Menu on a remote Apple: Settings › Screens › Home tiles (Apple TV keeps a fixed layout) Areas This screen Which areas the top bar offers. Home is always there. Space · House · Night · Status, all on Wall/TV: Settings › Home or Screens Apple: Settings › Screens › Areas Alarm and wake With Up by on Automatic, the up-by time is the earliest of: your first timed event minus the ready lead, and the wake time plus the snooze window. Snooze is refused when the next liftoff would be under 3 minutes before it. Setting What it does Choices Where Alarm House The master switch for the launch alarm. Armed · Off Wall/TV: Night Apple: Night, Goodnight Wake time House · per day Your weekly wake time, by day group. ±15 min, or an exact time Wall/TV: Night, or Settings › Night & alarm › Schedule Apple: Night › Schedule Alarm on this day House · per day Whether that day rings. On · Off Wall/TV: Night, Schedule Apple: Schedule Customize days Your view Edit Mon–Fri as one group, or each day on its own. Mon–Fri · Sat · Sun ; splits by itself when days differ Wall/TV: Schedule Apple: Schedule Tomorrow only House A one-time wake change. The weekly plan stays as it is. ±15 min · Use weekly plan clears it Wall/TV: Night › Tomorrow only, Schedule Apple: Night, Goodnight Who’s launching House · per day The whole crew gets the full bedroom alarm; one crew member gets a quiet alarm on their own phone. Everyone · one crew member Wall/TV: Night Apple: Night › Crew & snooze defaults Up by House · per day The latest you can snooze to that day. ±15 min · Automatic Wall/TV: Night Apple: Night › Crew & snooze defaults Snooze House Minutes per snooze, followed by a one-minute count. 3–20 min · 10 Wall/TV: Used, not editable yet Apple: Night › Crew & snooze defaults Snooze window House With Up by on Automatic, you must be up within this long of the wake time. 0 means no snoozing. 0–120 min · 30 Wall/TV: Used, not editable yet Apple: Night › Crew & snooze defaults Ready lead House With Up by on Automatic, you must be up this long before your first timed event. 0–120 min · 45 Wall/TV: Used, not editable yet Apple: Night › Crew & snooze defaults Alarm volume limit House The loudest the alarm takes a bedroom speaker, if you have one. 0–100 · 30 Wall/TV: Settings › Night & alarm Apple: Night › Sleep sound › Alarm max volume Liftoff level This screen The loudness of the liftoff rumble only. −6 to +6 dB · 0 Wall/TV: Settings › Display Apple: Not on Apple yet This screen’s own alarm This screen A backup alarm on the screen itself, used when Home Assistant isn’t connected. 6:30 AM, on Wall/TV: Settings › Display (hidden when Home Assistant is connected) Apple: — Night and Goodnight Setting What it does Choices Where Bedtime target House · per day The target for the night before each wake day. It never starts anything. A time per group · wake − 8 h Wall/TV: Schedule Apple: Schedule Sleep sound House Plays all night from Goodnight on the speaker or screen that plays it. Thunderstorm · Rain · Off Wall/TV: Night Apple: Night, Goodnight Sleep volume House The live volume of the bedroom speaker. 0–100 · 20 Wall/TV: Night (− / +) Apple: Night, Goodnight, Watch At night This screen What the screen does at Goodnight. Off (screen off) · Dim (a night clock) · Stay on Wall/TV: Settings › Night & alarm or Screens Apple: Settings › Screens When woken This screen What a touch or key shows at night, for ten seconds. Automatic (pure black on TVs, night clock on the panel) · Pure black · Night clock Wall/TV: Settings › Night & alarm, Night, Screens Apple: Settings › Screens ( Pure black ) Night brightness This screen How bright a Dim screen is. 1% , up to 30% "},{"t":"What am I looking at?","u":"/docs/screens/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. A guide to each area’s headline readouts and Flight Day’s status lines: “as of”, dimmed tiles, waiting and offline states, snoozed and muted lines, demo labels.","h":[["areas","The headline in each area"],["freshness","Fresh, old and missing"],["alarm","The alarm"],["house","The house"],["labels","Labels and badges"],["coming","Coming later"]],"x":"What am I looking at? Flight Day shows real data or says plainly what it’s waiting for. A missing value is never a default, and an old value carries its age. The headline in each area Area Look here first Home The big clock and the line under it: the next event, the wake time or a launch’s T-minus. Then Weather, and Today or Tomorrow. Space The top row of the manifest, with its T-minus, and the brief under the globe. House The lights that are on, what’s playing, and the thermostat’s mode and setpoint. Night Tomorrow’s wake time, whether the alarm is armed, and Tonight: what Goodnight will do. Status What’s connected and what isn’t, and plan limits. Fresh, old and missing You see It means “as of 6:10 AM” on a dimmed tile The data is older than it should be. It’s the last value received, not a fresh reading. A standby line, such as “Thermostat not connected” or “Calendar not connected” That source isn’t connected. Nothing is filled in for it. “Not connected” in Status, with a list The sources that have no connection, each with a link to the setting that fixes it. “Home Assistant · not connected — add URL + token in Settings” No house is set up on this screen. “This card could not load · try again from Settings → Reload” A tile failed to draw. “House action not confirmed · reconnecting” A command didn’t come back confirmed. Don’t assume it worked. The alarm You see It means “T-04:12 · liftoff 6:50 AM” The count to your wake time. “liftoff 6:50 AM · up by 7:20 AM · 1 snooze” The alarm header: liftoff, the up-by time and snoozes used so far. “Snoozed · liftoff 7:00 AM”, or “snoozing · liftoff …” at night Waiting to count down again. “No more snoozes — up by …” The up-by time is too close for another snooze. “Final launch” The last liftoff before up-by. I’m up needs a hold. “muted · the lights will still rise” The bedroom speaker is muted. Flight Day never unmutes it in the morning. “house offline — lights not held” The screen is running its own backup alarm, without the house. “Up … · no answer” Nobody answered; the alarm stopped after 15 minutes. The house You see It means “Comfort · waiting for the house” or “Comfort offline” No comfort status has arrived from Home Assistant yet. “Room sensors · waiting for Apple Home” No room readings have arrived yet. “· not counted” on a dimmed room That reading is too old, or the room is left out, so comfort ignores it. “Comfort is quiet for the night” A bedroom screen at night: comfort controls wait until morning. “Choose Heat or Cool to see its setpoint” The thermostat is Off, so there’s no setpoint to show. “Needs a hand” The vacuum is stuck or needs maintenance. Labels and badges You see It means LIVE banner A launch is in flight. Watch opens it. Plan limits: live, standby, “report from …”, “limit hit”, “est.” The state of each AI plan’s usage report. A limit the service doesn’t report is never made up. A yellow “demo” chip That tile shows sample data, on purpose. “AI” on a line The line was made by AI. “Estimated · typical ascent, not telemetry” Flight Director’s altitude and speed are a typical ascent, not live data. Coming later Go / No-Go In development (in the demo house; arrives with the next app release): a line at the top of the briefing, for example “Checks incomplete · weather as of 6:10 AM”. Until then, the briefing shows each input with its own age."},{"t":"Things to try","u":"/docs/things-to-try/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. Ten quick wins in today’s Flight Day build: an alarm preview, the night screen, arranging Home, tracking a launch and more.","h":[],"x":"Things to try Quick wins in today’s build. None of them touch the house. Preview the alarm. Night › Tonight › Try the alarm runs a 45-second countdown and liftoff on that screen only. It’s also in Settings › Display. See the night screen. Night › Try night screen , then touch the screen to bring up the dim controls. Pick your night look. In the night controls, switch This screen between Pure black and Night clock. Arrange Home. Press and hold a Home tile (or press Menu on a TV remote) to hide, show and reorder tiles. Follow a launch. In Space, pick a launch and choose Track , then Watch to open Flight Director. Change the picture. Switch Ambient between Still, Animated and Live in Settings › Ambient. On a TV, Play/Pause turns Ambient on and off. Show both clocks. Settings › Display › time format Both shows “07:45 (7:45 AM)”. Open the briefing any time. Tap the Weather tile on the panel, or the Today tile on a TV. Set how long the briefing stays. Settings › Night & alarm › Briefing stays up: 1 h, 2 h, 3 h or until noon. Make one morning different. Night › Tomorrow only changes one wake time and leaves the week alone."},{"t":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"/docs/troubleshooting/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. Common Flight Day questions and honest fixes: a black screen at night, a missing Snooze, muted speakers, old values, a locked thermostat, missing weather and more.","h":[["night-black","The screen is black at night"],["first-press","My first press during the alarm did nothing"],["no-snooze","Snooze is missing"],["muted","It says “muted · the lights will still rise”"],["house-offline","It says “house offline — lights not held”"],["old-value","A tile is dimmed and says “as of …”"],["thermostat-locked","The thermostat shows “—” and − and + are locked"],["comfort-quiet","Comfort did nothing overnight"],["rooms-waiting","It says “Room sensors · waiting for Apple Home”"],["no-weather","There’s no weather, or no rain line"],["old-launcher","A TV shows its old launcher"],["sleep-sound","The sleep sound says “cannot be played right now · retrying”"],["apple-token","An Apple device asks for a token"],["control-missing","A control seems to be missing"],["dont","What not to do"]],"x":"Troubleshooting and FAQ Try the smallest useful check first. Flight Day never fills in a guess, so most fixes start with reading what the screen says. The screen is black at night That’s the night screen. One touch or key shows the dim controls for ten seconds. A Sony TV with Picture off uses the first key to turn its picture back on, so press a second key. My first press during the alarm did nothing The first key only shows the controls, so a sleepy press can’t cancel the morning. Only Back and Play/Pause act straight away, and both snooze. Snooze is missing The up-by time is too close for another snooze. Choose I’m up (on the final launch it needs a hold), or hold Scrub to call off the morning. It says “muted · the lights will still rise” The bedroom speaker is muted, and Flight Day won’t unmute it for you. Choose “Muted · Select to unmute” in the night controls if you want sound. Choosing Goodnight with a sleep sound also unmutes it. It says “house offline — lights not held” The screen can’t reach Home Assistant, so it’s running its own backup alarm, and a snooze affects only that screen. Check Status for what isn’t connected. A tile is dimmed and says “as of …” The data is old. Reconnecting usually refreshes it. An old value on screen isn’t proof that a command worked. The thermostat shows “—” and − and + are locked It’s Off. Choose Heat or Cool to get a setpoint, or Auto to hand it back to Smart Comfort. Comfort did nothing overnight A manual Off stays off until you choose Resume Auto . Smart Comfort’s automatic mode may also still be watching only. It says “Room sensors · waiting for Apple Home” No room readings are arriving. Check the automation that sends them. Don’t type numbers in by hand; Flight Day only uses real readings. There’s no weather, or no rain line Weather needs a home location: check Settings › Home location. A dry next hour shows no rain line on purpose, and bedroom screens hide rain hints at night. A TV shows its old launcher If Flight Day crashes three times within five minutes, a safety watchdog hands the TV back to its previous launcher for ten minutes. It comes back by itself. The sleep sound says “cannot be played right now · retrying” Flight Day keeps retrying. Check that the speaker is on and that the sound’s source can be reached. An Apple device asks for a token Enter it once in Settings on one device. With iCloud Keychain on, your other Apple devices get it too. A control seems to be missing Scroll the focused card or the Settings detail. A locked control needs its real source or device; Flight Day doesn’t show controls for things your house can’t do. What not to do Don’t factory-reset a screen, uninstall the app, clear its storage or re-pair the house as a routine fix. Those throw away working settings. If an update doesn’t go well, ask for help first."},{"t":"Requirements and setup","u":"/docs/requirements/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. What runs Flight Day today, what’s optional, and what isn’t built yet. The install guide is being prepared.","h":[["today","What runs it today"],["optional","Optional, when you have them"],["not-built","Not built yet"],["privacy","Privacy"]],"x":"Requirements and setup An overview of what Flight Day runs on today. The install guide is being prepared. Not available to install yet. Flight Day runs in one household today, set up by hand. The iPhone, iPad and Apple Watch apps are in private TestFlight testing; Mac and Apple TV are in development. There is no installer for other homes yet; it’s in development. What runs it today Home Assistant keeps the wake schedule, the alarm and the house in one place, and keeps every screen in step. Screens: an Android wall panel, Google TVs, with iPhone, iPad and Apple Watch in private TestFlight testing. Mac and Apple TV apps are in development. The alarm sound plays on a screen; on your chosen alarm screen. Flight Day Hub (optional), on a computer in the home. It shares one space-data cache, the next-hour rain forecast, screen settings, Track and sleep summaries between screens. Without it, each screen uses the public feeds directly. Optional, when you have them A bedroom speaker, smart lights, a thermostat, a robot vacuum, cameras and room sensors, through Home Assistant AccuWeather or Apple Weather in Home Assistant (Open-Meteo is built in) Calendars, and a usage report for plan limits See What you can add for what each one does and its status. Not built yet An installer and setup for other homes In development Pairing a new screen with your house In development Settings › Integrations, for adding devices from the app In development Solo flight : works without Home Assistant on one device, with the alarm, your calendar, weather and Space In development A demo house in the apps, to try it without a house In development (in your browser now: try the demo house ) Public app and hub downloads Privacy No accounts, no Flight Day cloud and no tracking. Your data stays in your house. See Privacy ."},{"t":"Words we use","u":"/docs/words/","g":"Reference","s":"In development. Flight Day’s space words and what each one means in plain terms: liftoff, Scrub, crew, Systems check, Mission Log, Solo flight and the rest, plus the places that always stay plain.","h":[["today","In the apps today"],["next","Arriving with the next app releases"],["plain","What always stays plain"]],"x":"Words we use Flight Day borrows a few words from spaceflight. Each one has a plain meaning, and the app shows that meaning next to it wherever it isn’t obvious. In the apps today Word What it means Liftoff The moment your alarm goes off: your wake time. See The launch alarm . Countdown T-minus · T-plus The ten minutes before liftoff while the screen brightens. T-minus is the time left; T-plus is the time since. Snooze Ten more minutes. The screen shows the new liftoff, for example “Snoozed · liftoff 7:57 AM”. I’m up You’re awake. The alarm stops and the morning briefing follows. Scrub Alarm off today Turns today’s alarm off. It’s a deliberate hold, so a sleepy tap can’t cancel the morning. Launch now Starts a ten-second final count, rather than skipping straight to liftoff. Back to bed Returns only the bedroom to night, until noon. Goodnight One tap dims the screens for the night. See Goodnight and the night screen . Briefing The morning summary after liftoff: weather, rain soon, your day and the next launch. See The morning briefing . Flight Director The screen that takes over near a featured launch, before its stream. See The Space feed . Crew People who live here The people who live in the home; each of them is a crew member. Who’s launching picks which of the crew the alarm wakes each day. See The sleep schedule . Labs Features you can try before they’re finished, off until you turn them on, such as Smart Comfort’s automatic heat and cool. Arriving with the next app releases These names are decided and arrive with the next app releases. Some of the features they name aren’t built yet, and say so. Word What it means Systems check What needs attention at home Batteries, offline devices, automation errors: anything at home that needs a hand. In development See Systems check . Caution · Warning How serious a Systems check finding is. Caution needs attention; Warning is a problem. When there’s nothing, it says “Nothing needs you”. Flight Log Your last 7 mornings When you got up each day, next to when the alarm was set. Today’s heading is Sleep, or Last 7 mornings. Cabin climate Smart Comfort or Thermostat The heading of the thermostat and comfort card in the House area. Comms Notices from Flight Day and your integrations The list of notices. Each notice itself stays plain. Pre-launch checklist Step 3 of 7 The progress line above each setup step. In development Solo flight This device only, no Home Assistant Works without Home Assistant on one device, with the alarm, your calendar, weather and Space. In development Mission Log What’s new in each release The What’s new page. This site’s is the Mission Log ; in the apps, the Settings row still says What’s new. Go / No-Go The morning verdict at the top of the briefing, with its reason, for example “Go. Dry until 6:00 PM”. It means nothing else in Flight Day. In development (in the demo house; arrives with the next app release) Debrief An evening look back: what got done, tomorrow’s first event and the wake time. In development Flight Plan Only ever the AI-made plan for your day. Your calendar stays Today and Tomorrow. Concept What always stays plain Settings rows, unless the plain meaning is on the same row. Permission prompts and system dialogs. Errors, and the labels that tell you what’s real: Real, Sample data, Concept, AI-made and “as of”. The Night area and the night screen: labels and values only. VoiceOver and TalkBack, which always read the plain meaning, for example “Turn off today’s alarm” for Scrub."}];
